  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and very humid conditions are expected to \npersist through Tuesday of next week. A Heat Advisory is in \neffect for portions of the area through Saturday.\n\nA relatively stagnant synoptic pattern is developing across our\nregion. Quasi zonal mid and upper level flow will be in place \nthrough the middle of next week as Bermuda high pressure \npersists offshore. This will keep a steady stream of very warm \nand moist air in place across the region through the period.\n\nA prolonged period of hot and, most notably, very humid \nconditions is expected to remain in place for the next several \ndays. We look to have a weak cold frontal passage Sunday, \nushering in slightly drier air. This will give some slight \nreprieve from the excessive humidity, though highs will still be\nin the upper 80s to low 90s. Moisture advection will return \nagain by Monday though, and hot and very humid conditions are \nanticipated to return Monday through Tuesday. A somewhat \nstronger cold front looks to come through late Tuesday or \nWednesday, which will help break the excessive heat and humidity\nsome.\n\nA Heat Advisory remains in effect for portions of eastern \nPennsylvania, much of New Jersey except the far southern \ncounties, as well as New Castle County, Delware. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...There are daily chances for showers and \nthunderstorms through Saturday, as well as portions of next \nweek. There is at least a marginal risk each day for localized \nheavy rainfall leading to flash flooding and locally damaging \nwind gusts with any stronger thunderstorms that develop.\n\nOn Saturday, the extent of the convection remains more unclear.\nThe current guidance shows isolated convection developing \nduring the later afternoon into the evening hours. PWATs look \nslightly lower than today, and mean flow is a bit more westerly \nthan southwesterly, but overall the thermodynamics appear \nsimilar. There will be less forcing, so the coverage is more \nisolated compared to today. We remain in a Marginal (level 1 out\nof 4) risk for excessive rainfall from WPC. In terms of the \nsevere weather potential, SPC has added a Marginal (level 1 out \nof 5) risk for isolated severe thunderstorms for most of our \narea with the primary hazard being damaging wind gusts. \n\nWe look to have a weak cold frontal passage by Sunday, ushering\nin slightly drier air. This combined with subtle subsidence \naloft will help briefly limit convection potential on Sunday. \nMoisture and forcing aloft will return by Monday as mean flow \nshifts more westerly and mid level flow increases. This should \nresult in a greater severe thunderstorm threat compared to this \nweek, and perhaps slightly lower flash flooding threat, but the \ndetails into the period early next week remain unclear. Long \nstory short, Monday and Tuesday look to continue the hot and \nhumid conditions and daily thunderstorm chances.\n\nA somewhat stronger cold front looks to come through late \nTuesday or Wednesday, which will help break the excessive heat \nand humidity some, but the pattern aloft may still support \ndiurnally driven convection.",
